# Line endings.
#
# Windows installs of Git default to core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites LF to CRLF on
# checkout. For most files that is invisible and fine. For these it is not:
#
#   * scripts/docker-entrypoint.sh is COPYied into a Linux image and run by /bin/sh. With
#     CRLF, the shebang line reads `#!/usr/bin/env sh\r` and the kernel looks for an
#     interpreter literally named "sh\r" — so the container exits immediately with
#     `/usr/bin/env: 'sh\r': No such file or directory`, which names no file in this
#     repository and appears only on Windows. The image builds cleanly; it just cannot run.
#   * The same applies to every script under scripts/, which Git Bash executes directly.
#   * Dockerfile RUN lines with trailing \r break the shell inside the build.
#
# `eol=lf` overrides autocrlf per path, so a Windows clone gets exactly the bytes Linux
# gets for these files and CRLF stays available everywhere else.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.sh          text eol=lf
Dockerfile    text eol=lf
Makefile      text eol=lf
*.yml         text eol=lf
*.yaml        text eol=lf

# Real binaries: never touched.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.pdf binary
*.ico binary
